WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.139
Film Courage: Is there anything you want your
students to know before they start writing

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:04.139
a screenplay?

00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:05.139
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:05.139 --> 00:00:07.430
Media Arts: Before they begin writing a screenplay
what should they know?

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.740
Is there anything they should know before
writing a screenplay?

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.940
Yes I guess there is one.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:21.220
Am I passionate enough about this project
to spend six months on it?

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.010
Do I really care that much?

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:28.150
Because it’s going to be tough sledding
at some point.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:32.432
In fact my old mentor had a couple of suggestions
[Frank Daniel].

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:33.809
I forget if I shared these?

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:39.550
He had a couple of interesting suggestions
for how to deal with when the going gets really

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.540
rough.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:49.399
He said that when you get your inspiration
for this script and it’s going to be the

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:52.239
greatest script anybody ever wrote.

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:55.180
Then what you do it you type it out on a piece
of paper.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.160
Type it out…we don’t do that.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.199
Write it out on a computer and print it out
later - what your reviews are going to be,

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:07.249
what your speech is going to be at the Academy
Awards, what historians are going to say about

00:01:07.249 --> 00:01:09.520
this script and how great it is.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.729
Put all of that on a piece of paper and you
seal it in a envelope and you put it away

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:15.719
somewhere carefully.

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:22.189
Then when you’re writing it and you’re
in the middle of it and everything is bad.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:24.119
Every scene you write tastes like yesterday’s
oatmeal.

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:25.119
Ah!

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:27.659
It doesn’t work and you say “I’m done.

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:28.859
I’m giving up.”

00:01:28.859 --> 00:01:33.649
He said that is when you reach for your envelope
because it reconnects you with what that initial

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:35.259
inspiration was.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:40.009
Now in my experience doing that I never reached
for…I was always like “What if that fails?

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:41.099
I better not reach for it.

00:01:41.099 --> 00:01:42.670
I’ll keep going.”

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.599
But knowing it was there helped.

00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:45.599
Ah!

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:47.739
That’s not working.

00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:56.079
So before they begin are they passionate enough
about the subject matter to whether the pain

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.579
it’s going to cause to do it.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:01.240
Film Courage: I like that idea.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:07.709
So right when there is a spark and you’re
just so into it writing (not the script) you

00:02:07.709 --> 00:02:12.209
are writing reviews, your acceptance speech,
you’re up there, you’re in your dress

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:18.000
or tux or whatever and you’re thanking everyone
and how [your script] has changed the course

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.000
of culture, whatever…

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Paul: The culture of cinema.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.189
Film Courage: Yes and then you keep that there
[the envelope tucked away]…wow interesting.

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:25.189
Great tool.

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:26.250
Paul: It is very interesting.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.319
They also said don’t write to the edge.

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:34.120
When you’re writing scenes.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:41.150
If you’ve got the idea of how the scene
will end don’t finish it…stop, make a

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.170
few notes about how you’re going to finish
it and that way when you pick up the next

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.049
day you’re not starting over again.

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:51.530
You’re like “Oh that’s where I was”
and you can pick up where you left off.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:57.350
So never write all the way to where you never
have any more ideas.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.099
Stop just before then and keep plowing ahead
to the next time.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:08.129
So that’s something I do all of the time
to make sure I know what is coming next.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:12.660
And he said don’t write if you’re not
excited.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.379
Whatever your technique is whether you are
listening to music, getting up and pacing

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:17.379
around the room.

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:21.920
But get excited about what you are doing because
that will show through with what you’re

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:22.920
doing.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:23.480
The passion comes through.

